Myth: Long ago, the Night was not empty but filled with sorrow. She wept because mortals feared her, hiding behind locked doors when she arrived each evening.

A young shepherd named Kael understood her loneliness. Unlike others, he welcomed the darkness, for it brought cool relief from the scorching Sun. One evening, he called up to the Night, "Why do you weep?"

"I bring only fear," she whispered. "No one sees beauty in me."

Kael thought deeply. He gathered embers from his dying fire and seeds of light from the Moon's reflection in a still pond. Working through the night, he planted them in the soil of the highest hill, whispering songs of comfort.

By dawn, luminous flowers bloomed—but they were not meant for day. When Night returned, the flowers released their seeds, which drifted upward and took root in the sky itself, becoming thousands of gentle stars.

The Night laughed for the first time, her tears becoming silver moonbeams. She blessed Kael with peace in darkness.

From then on, humans no longer feared the Night, for they saw she was not empty but tenderly adorned. And Kael's flowers bloom still, releasing new stars each century to replace those that fade.
